`down(n)` is pure recursion with no loop anywhere in it (confirmed by
inspection: one `if`, one recursive call, no `while`/`for`), so codegen emits
no `bit_rt_safepoint` at any back-edge THIS PROGRAM owns — that half of the
original claim holds.

But `BIT_GC=stress` does not report zero: 5/5 runs measured `collections=103`,
2 above stress.bit's documented scheduler-loop floor of ~101
(runtime/sched/workerrun.bit's `Worker.run`, which every worker OS thread
keeps running regardless of what this program does). The +2 is plausibly the
`chan<int>` send/receive and the one green-task spawn this program performs —
both go through scheduler paths that are not `@nosplit` — not re-derived
further here. Either way, none of those collections fire inside `down` itself,
so the collection count still verifies nothing about the stack-depth budget
this program measures.

A loop must NOT be added to earn the oracle. The assertion is a stack byte
budget divided by a frame size, and anything that changes the frame — a live
loop counter, an extra local, a rooted temporary — changes the depth this
program can reach without changing what it claims to measure.
